S="Give Thanks"
D("2026-11-23",S,"The Walls of Jericho","a 3rd–5th grader","Joshua 1:9",[(9,"Have I not commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Do not be frightened, and do not be dismayed, for the LORD your God is with you wherever you go.”")],
"Joshua and Jericho",[
"After forty years in the desert, Moses died, and God chose Joshua to lead His people into the promised land. God told him, “Be strong and courageous. I will be with you wherever you go.” But the first city in their way was Jericho, with huge, thick walls and locked gates.",
"God gave Joshua a strange battle plan. “March around the city once a day for six days, with seven priests blowing trumpets in front of the ark. Don’t say a word. On the seventh day, march around seven times. Then the priests will blow a long blast, all the people will shout, and the walls will fall down.”",
"It sounded silly. Who wins a battle by walking? But Joshua and the people obeyed exactly. On the seventh day, after the seventh lap, the trumpets blasted, everyone shouted, and the great walls of Jericho came tumbling down flat! God won the battle, just as He promised."],
"God’s way works",["God’s plan didn’t make sense to human eyes, but God was the one fighting. He often works through things that look weak or silly, like water in Baptism, bread and wine in the Lord’s Supper, and words read from a book. That’s where His power is."],
["How many days did they march? What happened when they shouted?","Why do you think God gave such a strange plan?","God works through “ordinary” things like water, bread, and words. Why do you think He does that?"],
"Mighty God, thank You for fighting for Your people at Jericho and for being with us wherever we go.","Make us strong and courageous, trusting Your way even when it seems strange.",
"God’s people marched around Jericho, blew trumpets, and shouted, and the walls came tumbling down!",
["God told Joshua, “Be strong and [courageous].”","They marched around the city for [seven] days.","The priests blew [trumpets].","The people gave a big [shout].","The [walls] fell down flat."],
["JOSHUA","JERICHO","WALLS","TRUMPETS","SHOUT","SEVEN"],[("Did the people knock the walls down with tools?",["Yes","No"],1),("Who won the battle?",["God","The walls"],0)],
"Draw the walls of Jericho falling down.",("Joshua 1:9","“Be strong and courageous… for the LORD your God is with you.”"),
scene(wall(330,620,150),person(130,beard=True,arms="mixed",hair="wrap"),trumpet(150,130),person(240,beard=False,arms="up",hair="short",s=.8),'<path d="M380,150 l10,-20 l14,10 M470,150 l8,-24 l16,12 M560,150 l12,-18 l10,14" fill="none" stroke-width="2"/>',label="Coloring picture: Joshua blowing trumpets at the walls of Jericho"))

D("2026-11-24",S,"Gideon’s Tiny Army","an older kid","Judges 6:12",[(12,"And the angel of the LORD appeared to him and said to him, “The LORD is with you, O mighty man of valor.”")],
"Gideon and the 300",[
"Later, enemies called the Midianites kept stealing all of Israel’s food. A young man named Gideon was hiding in a winepress, secretly threshing wheat, when an angel appeared and said, “The Lord is with you, mighty warrior!” Gideon looked around. Mighty warrior? Him? “I’m the weakest one in my family!”",
"But God said, “I will be with you.” Gideon gathered 32,000 soldiers. God said, “Too many! If you win, you’ll think you did it yourselves.” He sent home everyone who was afraid, then had the rest drink from a stream and kept only the 300 who lapped water with their hands. 300 men against an army as thick as locusts!",
"In the middle of the night, the 300 surrounded the enemy camp. Each man had a trumpet and a torch hidden inside a clay jar. At Gideon’s signal, they blew their trumpets, smashed their jars so the torches blazed, and shouted, “A sword for the Lord and for Gideon!” The enemy panicked, and the whole army ran away."],
"God’s strength in weakness",["God kept making Gideon’s army smaller so everyone would know the victory came from God. Paul says God’s “power is made perfect in weakness” (2 Corinthians 12:9). When we feel small, we’re in a good spot for God to work."],
["Where was Gideon hiding? What did the 300 men carry?","Why did God make the army smaller and smaller?","God called fearful Gideon a “mighty man.” What does that tell you about how God sees you?"],
"Lord God, thank You for using weak and small people to do Your work, so that You get the glory.","When we feel too small or scared, remind us that You are with us.",
"God used Gideon and only 300 men with trumpets, jars, and torches to win the battle!",
["Gideon was hiding in a [winepress].","The angel called him a mighty [warrior].","God made the army smaller, down to [300].","They hid torches inside clay [jars].","They blew their [trumpets] and shouted."],
["GIDEON","JARS","TORCHES","TRUMPETS","ANGEL","THREE"],[("Did Gideon feel strong at first?",["Yes","No"],1),("Why did God use only 300 men?",["So He got the glory","He ran out of men"],0)],
"Draw a clay jar with a torch inside, and a trumpet.",("Judges 6:12","“The LORD is with you, O mighty man of valor.”"),
scene(moon(560,56),stars(12,21,(160,480)),person(220,beard=False,arms="mixed",hair="short"),trumpet(250,130),jar(330),jar(390),jar(450),tent(560),label="Coloring picture: Gideon with trumpets and torches in clay jars"))

D("2026-11-25",S,"The One Who Came Back","a K–2nd grader, with help","Luke 17:15–16",[(15,"Then one of them, when he saw that he was healed, turned back, praising God with a loud voice;"),(16,"and he fell on his face at Jesus’ feet, giving him thanks. Now he was a Samaritan.")],
"Ten lepers",[
"One day, as Jesus walked into a village, ten men stood far away and called out to Him. They had a terrible skin disease called leprosy. Because people were afraid of catching it, they had to live outside town, away from their families. “Jesus, Master, have mercy on us!”",
"Jesus said, “Go and show yourselves to the priests.” That’s what you did after you got better. And as the ten men walked away, they looked down at their skin and saw they were healed! All ten were clean and healthy.",
"Nine of them kept going, probably running home to their families. But one man stopped, turned around, and ran back to Jesus, shouting praise to God. He fell at Jesus’ feet and said thank you. He was a Samaritan, a foreigner. Jesus asked, “Weren’t ten healed? Where are the other nine?” Then He told the man, “Get up. Your faith has made you well.”"],
"Thank you",["All ten were healed, but only one came back to say thank you. Tomorrow is Thanksgiving. God gives us so much every single day: food, family, home, and most of all Jesus. Saying thank you to God is a joyful way to live."],
["How many men were healed? How many came back to say thank you?","Why do you think the other nine didn’t come back?","Luther’s explanation of the First Article ends, “For all this it is my duty to thank and praise, serve and obey Him.” What are you most thankful for this year?"],
"Lord Jesus, thank You for all the ways You heal and help us. Forgive us for the times we forget to say thank you.","Give us thankful hearts tomorrow and every day.",
"Jesus healed ten sick men, but only one came back to say thank you!",
["[Ten] men were sick.","They had a disease called [leprosy].","Jesus [healed] them all.","Only one came back to say [thank] you.","He was a [Samaritan]."],
["THANKS","TEN","HEALED","LEPERS","PRAISE","NINE"],[("How many came back?",["One","Ten"],0),("Who should we thank for good gifts?",["God","Nobody"],0)],
"Make a list or drawing of ten things you are thankful for.",("Luke 17:15","“He turned back, praising God with a loud voice.”"),
scene(hills(),person(170,beard=True,arms="open",hair="short"),person(270,beard=True,arms="up",hair="wrap",s=.85),''.join(person(x,beard=b,arms="open",hair=h,s=.6) for x,b,h in [(420,True,"short"),(480,False,"wrap"),(540,True,"curly"),(600,False,"short")]),label="Coloring picture: one healed man thanking Jesus while others walk away"))

D("2026-11-26","Thanksgiving Day","Enter His Gates with Thanksgiving","Mom or Dad","Psalm 100:4–5",[(4,"Enter his gates with thanksgiving, and his courts with praise! Give thanks to him; bless his name!"),(5,"For the LORD is good; his steadfast love endures forever, and his faithfulness to all generations.")],
"The first Thanksgiving",[
"In 1620, a group of Christians called the Pilgrims sailed across the stormy Atlantic Ocean on a small ship called the Mayflower. They wanted a place where they could worship God freely. The trip took more than two months, and when they landed, winter was already coming.",
"That first winter was terrible. They didn’t have enough food or warm houses, and about half of them died. In the spring, a Native American man named Squanto, who spoke English, helped them. He taught them how to plant corn, where to fish, and how to survive in their new home.",
"In the fall of 1621, the harvest came in. The Pilgrims had food for the winter! They held a feast for three days with their Wampanoag neighbors, and they thanked God for His care. Even after a year of so much loss, they knew every good gift came from Him."],
"Steadfast love",["The Pilgrims gave thanks even after a hard year. Thanksgiving isn’t only for easy years. Psalm 100 tells us why we can always give thanks: “The Lord is good; His steadfast love endures forever.” His love never runs out."],
["What was the Pilgrims’ ship called? Who helped them?","Why did the Pilgrims give thanks even after a hard winter?","Psalm 100 says God’s faithfulness lasts “to all generations.” How has God been faithful to our family?"],
"Lord God, You are good, and Your steadfast love endures forever. Thank You for our food, our home, our family and friends, and most of all for Jesus.","Fill our hearts and our table with thankfulness today.",
"Happy Thanksgiving! The Lord is good, and His steadfast love endures forever!",
["The Pilgrims sailed on the [Mayflower].","[Squanto] taught them to plant corn.","They had a big [feast].","We enter His gates with [thanksgiving].","His steadfast [love] endures forever."],
["THANKS","PILGRIMS","FEAST","HARVEST","CORN","GOOD"],[("Does God’s love ever run out?",["Yes","No"],1),("Who gives us every good gift?",["God","Ourselves"],0)],
"Draw your family’s Thanksgiving table.",("Psalm 100:5","“For the LORD is good; his steadfast love endures forever.”"),
scene(window(470),person(110,beard=True,arms="open",hair="short"),person(540,beard=False,arms="open",hair="wrap"),table(170,480),'<ellipse cx="320" cy="232" rx="40" ry="14"/><path d="M290,228 q30,-30 60,0"/>'+bread(210)+'<circle cx="430" cy="236" r="10"/><circle cx="450" cy="236" r="10"/>',label="Coloring picture: a family at a Thanksgiving table"))

D("2026-11-27",S,"Hannah’s Prayer","a 3rd–5th grader","1 Samuel 1:27",[(27,"For this child I prayed, and the LORD has granted me my petition that I made to him.")],
"A mother’s prayer",[
"A woman named Hannah wanted a baby more than anything. Year after year, she had no children, and another woman made fun of her for it. Hannah was so sad she cried and couldn’t eat.",
"One year, at the house of the Lord, Hannah prayed with her whole heart. She was praying so hard that her lips moved but no sound came out. The priest, Eli, thought something was wrong with her. Hannah said, “I’m pouring out my soul to the Lord.” She promised that if God gave her a son, she would give him to serve God all his life.",
"Eli said, “Go in peace. May God give you what you asked.” And God did! Hannah had a baby boy and named him Samuel. When he was old enough, she brought him to Eli to grow up serving in God’s house, just as she had promised. Then she sang a song of praise: “My heart rejoices in the Lord!”"],
"Pour out your heart",["Hannah brought her deepest sadness to God and held nothing back. God loves it when we pour out our hearts to Him in prayer. He always hears, and He answers in the way that is best."],
["What did Hannah want more than anything? What did she name her son?","Why did Eli think something was wrong with Hannah? What was she really doing?","Hannah kept her promise even though it was hard. What does it look like to “pour out your heart” to God?"],
"Heavenly Father, thank You for hearing Hannah’s prayer, and for hearing ours, even the ones too deep for words.","Help us bring everything to You in prayer.",
"Hannah prayed with her whole heart for a baby. God answered, and she named him Samuel!",
["Hannah wanted a [baby].","She prayed at the house of the [Lord].","The priest’s name was [Eli].","Her son was named [Samuel].","She said, “For this child I [prayed].”"],
["HANNAH","PRAYER","SAMUEL","ELI","PROMISE","TEMPLE"],[("Did God hear Hannah’s silent prayer?",["Yes","No"],0),("Did Hannah keep her promise?",["Yes","No"],0)],
"Draw Hannah holding baby Samuel.",("1 Samuel 1:27","“For this child I prayed.”"),
scene(temple(),person(160,beard=False,arms="up",hair="wrap"),person(500,beard=True,arms="open",hair="short"),label="Coloring picture: Hannah praying at the house of the Lord"))

D("2026-11-28",S,"Speak, Lord","a K–2nd grader, with help","1 Samuel 3:10",[(10,"And the LORD came and stood, calling as at other times, “Samuel! Samuel!” And Samuel said, “Speak, for your servant hears.”")],
"God calls Samuel",[
"Little Samuel grew up in the house of the Lord, helping old Eli the priest. One night, while Samuel was lying down near the lamp of God, he heard a voice: “Samuel!” He jumped up and ran to Eli. “Here I am! You called me.” But Eli said, “I didn’t call you. Go back to bed.”",
"It happened again. “Samuel!” He ran to Eli. “Here I am!” “I didn’t call you, my son. Lie down.” Then a third time. This time Eli understood: it was the Lord calling the boy! He told Samuel, “If He calls you again, say, ‘Speak, Lord, for Your servant hears.’”",
"Samuel lay down, and the Lord came and called, “Samuel! Samuel!” This time Samuel answered, “Speak, for Your servant hears.” And God told him important things. Samuel grew up to be a great prophet. The Lord was with him, and all Israel knew Samuel spoke God’s Word."],
"Listening to God",["God doesn’t usually call out loud in the night anymore. Today He speaks to us through His Word, the Bible. Every night at devotions, we can say what Samuel said: “Speak, Lord, for Your servant hears.”"],
["How many times did God call Samuel? Who did Samuel think was calling?","What did Eli tell Samuel to say?","How does God speak to us today? What helps you listen?"],
"Lord, speak to us through Your Word, for Your servants are listening. Thank You for calling Samuel, and for calling each of us by name.","Help us listen to You and do what You say.",
"God called little Samuel in the night. Samuel said, “Speak, Lord, for Your servant hears!”",
["Samuel helped old [Eli].","He heard a voice call his [name].","He thought it was Eli, but it was the [Lord].","God called him [three] times before he understood.","Samuel said, “Speak, for your servant [hears].”"],
["SAMUEL","SPEAK","LISTEN","LAMP","CALL","NIGHT"],[("Who was calling Samuel?",["The Lord","Eli"],0),("How does God speak to us today?",["Through His Word","He doesn’t"],0)],
"Draw Samuel listening in the night.",("1 Samuel 3:10","“Speak, for your servant hears.”"),
scene(window(470),moon(530,84),'<path d="M90,300 V266 H330 V300 M90,266 q0,-20 30,-20 h40 v20" />','<ellipse cx="150" cy="258" rx="20" ry="12"/>',person(420,beard=True,arms="open",hair="short",s=.9),'<path d="M240,240 h16 v26 h-16 Z M248,240 q-6,-10 0,-18 q6,8 0,18"/>',label="Coloring picture: young Samuel in bed hearing God call"))

D("2026-11-29","Advent","Hosanna!","an older kid","Matthew 21:9",[(9,"And the crowds that went before him and that followed him were shouting, “Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!”")],
"The first Sunday of Advent",[
"Today is the first Sunday of Advent, the start of a new church year. Advent means “coming.” For four weeks we get ready for Jesus’ coming at Christmas, and for His coming again at the end of time. Many families light the first candle on an Advent wreath tonight.",
"The Gospel reading for the first Sunday in Advent is a surprise: it isn’t about baby Jesus. It’s about Palm Sunday! Jesus rode into Jerusalem on a humble donkey, just as the prophet Zechariah said the King would come. Crowds spread their coats and palm branches on the road and shouted, “Hosanna! Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord!”",
"“Hosanna” means “Save us now!” The people were welcoming their King. Advent starts here because it reminds us who the baby in the manger really is: the King who came to save us, riding humbly toward the cross."],
"Your King is coming",["Every Sunday at church, right before the Lord’s Supper, we sing the same words the crowd shouted: “Hosanna in the highest! Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord!” Because in the Supper, Jesus still comes to us."],
["What did Jesus ride into Jerusalem? What did the people shout?","What does “Hosanna” mean? What does “Advent” mean?","Why do you think the church begins Advent with Palm Sunday instead of the Christmas story?"],
"Lord Jesus, our King, we welcome You! Hosanna! Thank You for coming humbly to save us.","Prepare our hearts this Advent for Your coming.",
"Advent has begun! Jesus our King comes to us humbly, and we shout, “Hosanna!”",
["Advent means “[coming].”","Jesus rode on a [donkey].","People waved [palm] branches.","They shouted, “[Hosanna]!”","Hosanna means “Save us [now]!”"],
["ADVENT","HOSANNA","DONKEY","PALMS","KING","WREATH"],[("Does Advent mean “coming”?",["Yes","No"],0),("What did Jesus ride?",["A donkey","A horse"],0)],
"Draw an Advent wreath with one candle lit.",("Matthew 21:9","“Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!”"),
scene(palm(60),palm(590),person(170,beard=False,arms="up",hair="curly",s=.85),donkey(370),person(380,beard=True,arms="open",hair="short",s=.6),person(500,beard=True,arms="up",hair="wrap",s=.85),label="Coloring picture: Jesus on a donkey with palm branches and an Advent wreath"))
